\chapter{Expression Templates: Lazy
Trees}\label{ch15-expression-templates-lazy-trees}

An ordinary arithmetic expression such as \texttt{a + b * c} evaluates
eagerly: the compiler emits code that multiplies \texttt{b} by
\texttt{c}, stores the product in a temporary, adds \texttt{a}, and
returns the result. For scalar values this is exactly what the
programmer wants. For richer domain types --- symbolic scalars, vector
expressions, AST fragments, parser costs --- eager evaluation throws
away the \emph{shape} of the computation. The intermediate temporary has
no record that it was once a product; the addition only sees a value.
Once evaluated, the structure is gone.

Expression templates solve this problem at the type level. Instead of
computing a result, each operator application builds a small node that
\emph{records} the operation and refers to its operands. The full
expression \texttt{a + b * c - a} becomes a compile-time tree of nested
node types. Nothing is computed until the programmer explicitly asks for
a result by calling \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. At that
point a single recursive walk fuses the whole computation into one pass,
with no intermediate temporaries exposed to the caller.

MuDSL provides this machinery through the \texttt{ExprTemplates} feature
tag, the \texttt{ExprLike} concept, the \texttt{BinExpr} and
\texttt{UnaryExpr} node templates, and a set of overloaded arithmetic
operators. This chapter describes how the lazy tree is constructed. The
mechanics of evaluation --- \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, and
fusion --- are covered in detail in Chapter 16. The present chapter
focuses on the construction side: how a DSL type becomes an expression
leaf, how operators compose nodes, and what the resulting type encodes.

\section{The Problem: Eager Evaluation Loses
Structure}\label{ch15-the-problem-eager-evaluation-loses-structure}

Consider a DSL for vector arithmetic where each \texttt{VecDSL} object
stands for a symbolic vector of known length. If \texttt{operator+} for
two \texttt{VecDSL} objects returned a new \texttt{VecDSL} holding the
elementwise sum, then an expression like \texttt{a + b * c + a} would
allocate two or three intermediate vectors and discard them. The final
result would be correct, but every intermediate would have been
materialized, copied, and thrown away.

Worse, any later analysis --- constant folding, common-subexpression
elimination, code generation --- would have nothing to work with. The
intermediate vectors are opaque values; they do not remember that they
were once a sum or a product. To recover the structure, the library
would have to re-parse the expression from a string, which defeats the
purpose of an embedded DSL.

The expression-template technique preserves the structure by giving each
operator a return type that \emph{is} the structure. A sum is not a
value, it is a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} A,\allowbreak{} B\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The type carries the operation tag and the types of both operands; the
value carries references to the operands themselves. The tree exists in
the program's type system, where the compiler can see it, and in the
program's runtime object graph, where the evaluator can walk it.

\section{What an Expression Template
Is}\label{ch15-what-an-expression-template-is}

An expression template is a compile-time representation of an arithmetic
expression as a tree of operation nodes. Each node has a type that names
the operation
(\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}})
and the types of its operands. Leaves of the tree are the user's DSL
values; internal nodes are \texttt{BinExpr} or \texttt{UnaryExpr}
instances.

The defining property is laziness. Constructing a node performs no
arithmetic. The node merely stores (copies of, or references to) its
operands and remembers which operation to apply. Evaluation is deferred
until an explicit \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} call
traverses the tree and performs the computation. Because the tree is
built entirely at compile time through template instantiation, the
compiler can often inline the entire evaluation down to the same machine
code an eager expression would produce, but without exposing any
intermediate to the caller.

This is the same technique used by Blitz++, Boost.uBLAS, and Eigen.
MuDSL provides a small, self-contained implementation intended for
embedded DSLs whose leaves are user-defined domain types.

\section{The ExprTemplates Feature
Tag}\label{ch15-the-exprtemplates-feature-tag}

The \texttt{ExprTemplates} feature tag is the entry point. Like every
MuDSL feature tag (see Chapter 5), it is an empty struct with a nested
\texttt{Mixin} template that injects members into the CRTP base class
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived,\allowbreak{} Features.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(see Chapter 3). A DSL type opts into expression templates by listing
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates} among its feature
arguments.

\begin{code}
struct ExprTemplates
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// operator+ (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator+ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::plus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// operator- (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator- (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::minus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// operator* (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator* (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// operator/ (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator/ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::divides<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// Unary negation
    friend constexpr auto
    operator- (const Derived &e)
    {
      return UnaryExpr<std::negate<>, Derived>{ e };
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{Mixin} declares five \texttt{friend} functions, found
through argument-dependent lookup whenever at least one operand has type
\texttt{Derived}. Four of them are the binary arithmetic operators
\texttt{+}, \texttt{-}, \texttt{*}, \texttt{/\allowbreak{}}; the fifth
is unary negation. Each returns a freshly constructed node rather than a
computed value. The return type names \texttt{Derived} as the left
operand, so the node type is parameterized by the concrete DSL type ---
for example
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
when both operands are \texttt{Scalar}.

Because these operators are templates on the right-hand operand
\texttt{R}, the right operand need not have the same type as the left. A
\texttt{Scalar} may be added to a \texttt{BinExpr}, or to a
\texttt{UnaryExpr}, or even to a plain \texttt{int}. The only
requirement is that the eventual evaluation can resolve every operand to
a value, which the \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} machinery in
Chapter 16 handles via \texttt{if constexpr} dispatch.

\section{The ExprLike Concept}\label{ch15-the-exprlike-concept}

A type is not useful as an expression leaf unless the evaluator can
extract a value from it. MuDSL captures this requirement with the
\texttt{ExprLike} concept, defined in the utility-concepts section of
the header (see Chapter 5).

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
concept ExprLike = HasFeature<T, ExprTemplates>
                   && requires (const T &t) { t.expr_value (); };
\end{code}

Two conditions must hold. First, \texttt{T} must mix in the
\texttt{ExprTemplates} feature, which \texttt{HasFeature} checks by
testing whether \texttt{T} is derived from
\texttt{ExprTemplates:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Second, \texttt{T} must expose a \texttt{const}-qualified
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member
that returns the underlying value. Both conditions are structural; the
concept verifies the \emph{shape} of the type, not the meaning of its
value.

The \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
accessor is the contract a leaf type fulfills. Whatever it returns --- a
\texttt{double}, a vector, an AST node, a parser result --- is what the
evaluator will feed into the operation tag. By keeping the return type
unconstrained, MuDSL leaves the value domain entirely to the user. The
same machinery serves a scalar calculator, a symbolic vector DSL, and a
parser-cost estimator; only the leaf type and its
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} change.

Note that \texttt{ExprLike} is not used to constrain the operator
templates in the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin. Those operators accept
any \texttt{Derived} on the left (they are found via ADL on
\texttt{Derived}) and any \texttt{R} on the right. \texttt{ExprLike} is
instead available for users who wish to write their own generic
algorithms over expression leaves, or to constrain their own DSL
extension points. The concept is descriptive of a leaf type, not
prescriptive of the operator set.

\section{A Minimal Leaf: The Scalar
Type}\label{ch15-a-minimal-leaf-the-scalar-type}

The simplest concrete leaf is a scalar wrapper around a \texttt{double}.
It derives from
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}Scalar,\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to inherit the operator mix-in, stores a \texttt{double}, and exposes
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} to return
it.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

int main() {
  Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
  auto expr = a + b * c - a;
  std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // 14
}
\end{code}

This is the example shipped as
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}12-expression-te\allowbreak{}mplates.\allowbreak{}cpp}.
The line \texttt{auto expr = a + \allowbreak{}b * c - a;} constructs a
lazy tree and binds it to \texttt{expr} without performing any
arithmetic. The subsequent
\texttt{expr.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} walks the
tree and prints \texttt{14}, since
\texttt{2 + 3*4 - 2 = 14\allowbreak{}}.

Observe the use of \texttt{auto}. The type of \texttt{expr} is a deeply
nested \texttt{BinExpr} instantiation; spelling it out by hand is
impractical and unnecessary. The chapter returns to this point in the
section on pitfalls below.

\section{The BinExpr Node}\label{ch15-the-binexpr-node}

Each binary operator produces a \texttt{BinExpr}. The node template is
parameterized by an operation tag, a left-operand type, and a
right-operand type. The node stores one instance of each operand, taken
by value in the constructor.

\begin{code}
template <typename Op, typename L, typename R> struct BinExpr
{
  L lhs;
  R rhs;

  constexpr BinExpr (L l, R r) : lhs (std::move (l)), rhs (std::move (r)) {}

  constexpr auto
  eval () const
  {
    auto l = eval_operand (lhs);
    auto r = eval_operand (rhs);
    return Op{}(l, r);
  }
  // ...
};
\end{code}

The two data members \texttt{lhs} and \texttt{rhs} hold the operands
\emph{by value}. When the left operand is a \texttt{Scalar}, the node
contains a copy of that \texttt{Scalar}. When the left operand is itself
a \texttt{BinExpr}, the node contains a copy of that inner node, which
in turn contains copies of its operands. The tree is a value: copying
the root copies the entire structure.

Storing operands by value is the design choice that makes expressions
safe to return from functions and store in variables. A node never
dangles a reference at a stack variable that has gone out of scope,
because it does not hold references at all --- it holds copies. (The
trade-off is copying cost, which the chapter returns to below.) The
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}move} in the constructor allows
callers to move rvalue operands into the node rather than copying.

The \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member is
\texttt{constexpr}, so a fully constructed expression can be evaluated
at compile time when its operands are literal types. The example above
could in principle be made \texttt{constexpr} and evaluated in a
\texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert}. The
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} helper, described in Chapter 16,
dispatches on whether the operand is itself evaluable (a node), an
\texttt{ExprLike} leaf, or a plain value.

\section{The UnaryExpr Node}\label{ch15-the-unaryexpr-node}

Unary operations produce a \texttt{UnaryExpr}, which stores a single
operand. The only unary operation currently provided is negation, which
uses
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
as its operation tag.

\begin{code}
template <typename Op, typename E> struct UnaryExpr
{
  E operand;

  explicit constexpr UnaryExpr (E e) : operand (std::move (e)) {}

  constexpr auto
  eval () const
  {
    if constexpr (requires { operand.eval (); })
      return Op{}(operand.eval ());
    else if constexpr (requires { operand.expr_value (); })
      return Op{}(operand.expr_value ());
    else
      return Op{}(operand);
  }
};
\end{code}

\texttt{UnaryExpr} mirrors \texttt{BinExpr}: a tag, an operand, a
\texttt{constexpr} constructor, and an
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} that recursively resolves its
operand and applies the operation. The dispatch logic is duplicated
inline rather than factored through \texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand},
but the effect is the same --- a node can be negated, a leaf can be
negated, and a plain value can be negated.

Applying unary negation to a \texttt{Scalar} produces a
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Applying it to a \texttt{BinExpr} produces a
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
nesting the existing tree inside the negation. Because the operator
returns by value, the resulting node owns its subtree completely.

\section{How the Operators Compose}\label{ch15-how-the-operators-compose}

The arithmetic operators are the construction site of the lazy tree.
Each operator call instantiates one node and returns it. Composition
arises because the return type of one operator becomes the operand type
of the next.

Consider the expression \texttt{b * c}. Both operands are
\texttt{Scalar}. The \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin's \texttt{operator*}
is found via ADL on the left operand and instantiated with
\texttt{Derived = Scalar\allowbreak{}} and \texttt{R = Scalar}. It
returns
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
constructed from copies of \texttt{b} and \texttt{c}. No multiplication
has occurred; the node merely holds the two scalars and the tag
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

Now consider \texttt{a + b * c}. The right operand of \texttt{+} is the
\texttt{BinExpr} just constructed. The mixin's \texttt{operator+} is
instantiated with \texttt{Derived = Scalar\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{R = BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
It returns
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The type literally reads as ``the sum of a \texttt{Scalar} and the
product of two \texttt{Scalar}s''. The structure of the expression is
encoded in the type.

Finally, \texttt{a + b * c - a} subtracts \texttt{a} from the previous
result. Here the left operand is a \texttt{BinExpr}, not a
\texttt{Scalar}. The mixin's \texttt{operator-} is found via ADL on
\texttt{Derived = Scalar\allowbreak{}}, so it cannot apply when the left
operand is a \texttt{BinExpr}. MuDSL therefore provides a second set of
free-function overloads that handle a \texttt{BinExpr} on the left.

\begin{code}
template <typename Op1, typename L1, typename R1, typename R>
constexpr auto
operator+ (const BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1> &lhs, const R &rhs)
{
  return BinExpr<std::plus<>, BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1>, R>{ lhs, rhs };
}

template <typename Op1, typename L1, typename R1, typename R>
constexpr auto
operator- (const BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1> &lhs, const R &rhs)
{
  return BinExpr<std::minus<>, BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1>, R>{ lhs, rhs };
}

template <typename Op1, typename L1, typename R1, typename R>
constexpr auto
operator* (const BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1> &lhs, const R &rhs)
{
  return BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1>, R>{ lhs, rhs };
}

template <typename Op1, typename L1, typename R1, typename R>
constexpr auto
operator/ (const BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1> &lhs, const R &rhs)
{
  return BinExpr<std::divides<>, BinExpr<Op1, L1, R1>, R>{ lhs, rhs };
}
\end{code}

These four overloads accept any \texttt{BinExpr} as the left operand and
any type \texttt{R} as the right. They wrap the pair in a fresh
\texttt{BinExpr} whose left type is the inner \texttt{BinExpr}. The
subtraction in \texttt{a + b * c - a} therefore produces
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}minus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
--- a left-leaning tree whose root is a minus, whose left child is the
prior sum, and whose right child is the leaf \texttt{a}.

The tree is left-leaning because all binary operators in C++ associate
left-to-right. The expression \texttt{a - b - c} parses as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}a - b)\allowbreak{} - c}, and the resulting node
type reflects that grouping. For commutative operations like addition
and multiplication the grouping is mathematically irrelevant; for
non-commutative operations like subtraction and division it determines
the order of evaluation. Programmers who need a different grouping must
parenthesize explicitly, which produces the corresponding nested node
type.

\section{Building a Tree Step by
Step}\label{ch15-building-a-tree-step-by-step}

It is instructive to watch a tree grow one operator at a time. Start
with three leaves.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
\end{code}

The first operation, \texttt{b * c}, yields a node of type
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The node's two members are copies of \texttt{b} and \texttt{c}; the
operation tag is the empty type
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
No arithmetic has been performed.

\begin{code}
auto bc = b * c;            // BinExpr<multiplies<>, Scalar, Scalar>
\end{code}

The next operation, \texttt{a + bc}, invokes the mixin's
\texttt{operator+} with \texttt{Derived = Scalar\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{R = decltype(\allowbreak{}bc)\allowbreak{}}. The result is a
deeper node.

\begin{code}
auto abc = a + bc;          // BinExpr<plus<>, Scalar, BinExpr<multiplies<>, Scalar, Scalar>>
\end{code}

The final operation, \texttt{abc - a}, invokes the free-function
\texttt{operator-} overload for a \texttt{BinExpr} left operand. The
result is deeper still.

\begin{code}
auto expr = abc - a;
// BinExpr<minus<>,
//   BinExpr<plus<>, Scalar, BinExpr<multiplies<>, Scalar, Scalar>>,
//   Scalar>
\end{code}

Each binding stores a complete value of the named type. The leaves
\texttt{a}, \texttt{b}, \texttt{c} are copied into the innermost nodes;
the inner nodes are copied into their parents; the root \texttt{expr}
owns the entire structure. Evaluating
\texttt{expr.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} walks from
the root down to the leaves, applying each operation tag in post-order.
Chapter 16 traces this walk in detail.

\section{The Type Encodes the Shape}\label{ch15-the-type-encodes-the-shape}

The most important property of an expression-template type is that it
records the shape of the expression. The type
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is, in effect, a small abstract syntax tree printed in template syntax.
The operation tag at each position names the node, and the operand types
name the children.

This property has several consequences. First, two expressions that
compute the same value but have different shapes have different types.
\texttt{a + b * c} and \texttt{a + c * b} are not the same type, even
when \texttt{b} and \texttt{c} are equal at runtime, because the operand
order in the multiplication differs. The compiler treats them as
distinct types and will not, for example, allow one to be assigned to
the other.

Second, the type is available for compile-time introspection. A generic
algorithm can constrain on \texttt{BinExpr} instantiations, inspect the
operation tag, or recurse into the operand types. Template
metaprogramming over expression types is possible, though MuDSL does not
impose any particular metaprogramming interface; the node templates are
intentionally simple structs whose members are public.

Third, the type is usually too complex to write by hand. A modest
expression of six operators can easily produce a type name over a
hundred characters long. The idiomatic way to bind an expression is
\texttt{auto}, which deduces the full type without requiring the
programmer to spell it. Where the type must be named --- for example, as
a function parameter or a class member --- a trailing return type or a
type alias is used.

\section{A Vector Leaf: Beyond
Scalars}\label{ch15-a-vector-leaf-beyond-scalars}

The \texttt{Scalar} example is the simplest possible leaf, but
expression templates are not limited to scalars. Any type that mixes in
\texttt{ExprTemplates} and exposes
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
participates in the same machinery. Consider a small vector DSL whose
leaves stand for elementwise vectors.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

struct VecDSL : dsl::DSL<VecDSL, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  std::array<double, 3> v{};
  VecDSL() = default;
  VecDSL(double x, double y, double z) : v{x, y, z} {}
  std::array<double, 3> expr_value() const { return v; }
};

std::array<double, 3> operator+(std::array<double, 3> a, std::array<double, 3> b) {
  return {a[0]+b[0], a[1]+b[1], a[2]+b[2]};
}
std::array<double, 3> operator*(std::array<double, 3> a, std::array<double, 3> b) {
  return {a[0]*b[0], a[1]*b[1], a[2]*b[2]};
}

int main() {
  VecDSL a{1, 2, 3}, b{4, 5, 6}, c{7, 8, 9};
  auto expr = a + b * c;            // lazy tree
  auto r = expr.eval();             // single fused pass
  std::cout << r[0] << ' ' << r[1] << ' ' << r[2] << "\n";  // 29 42 57
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{VecDSL} leaf returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}array\textless{}\allowbreak{}double,\allowbreak{} 3\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
\texttt{BinExpr} \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} applies
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to those arrays, which in turn requires overloads of \texttt{operator+}
and \texttt{operator*} for
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}array}. The point is that the
leaf type completely determines the value domain; the
expression-template machinery is value-agnostic.

Because evaluation is fused, the expression \texttt{a + b * c} allocates
no intermediate arrays visible to the caller. The
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} calls resolve each leaf to an array,
and the operation tags produce the final array in a single composition.
For larger vectors or more complex expressions, this is the central
benefit of the technique.

\section{Storing an Expression for Later
Evaluation}\label{ch15-storing-an-expression-for-later-evaluation}

A common pattern is to build an expression in one part of a program and
evaluate it in another. Because the node types are regular value types,
an expression can be stored in a variable, returned from a function, or
held in a container.

\begin{code}
auto make_expr(Scalar a, Scalar b, Scalar c) {
  return a + b * c - a;
}

Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
auto e = make_expr(a, b, c);   // type encodes the whole expression
// ... later, perhaps in a different scope ...
double result = e.eval();
\end{code}

The function \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}expr} returns a deeply nested
\texttt{BinExpr} by value. Move semantics make the return cheap: the
inner nodes are moved into the return value rather than copied. The
caller binds the result with \texttt{auto} and evaluates it later.
Between construction and evaluation the expression is a pure value
carrying the structure of the computation.

This pattern is the basis of deferred query languages, symbolic
calculators, and code generators. The expression is a first-class
object: it can be passed to functions, stored in data structures,
serialized (with appropriate support), and evaluated zero, one, or many
times. Each \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} call walks the
tree afresh; the tree itself is not consumed.

\section{Repeated Evaluation}\label{ch15-repeated-evaluation}

Because \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is a \texttt{const}
member that does not mutate the tree, an expression can be evaluated
repeatedly. This is useful when the leaves are mutable: change a leaf,
re-evaluate, observe a new result without rebuilding the tree.

There is a subtlety. The nodes store their operands by value, so the
tree holds \emph{copies} of the leaves, not references to them. Changing
the original \texttt{Scalar a} after the tree is built does not affect
the copy inside the tree. To observe changes, the leaf type itself must
be made to share state --- for example by holding its value through a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}shared\_\allowbreak{}ptr} --- or
the expression must be rebuilt after each change.

For most DSL applications this is acceptable. Expressions are built,
evaluated once, and discarded. Where repeated evaluation against
changing leaves is required, the leaf type can be designed to expose a
level of indirection, but that is a property of the leaf, not of the
expression-template machinery.

\section{Constexpr Potential}\label{ch15-constexpr-potential}

Every node constructor and every
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member is declared
\texttt{constexpr}. This means an expression whose leaves are literal
types can be constructed and evaluated at compile time, producing a
constant result with no runtime cost.

\begin{code}
constexpr double square_plus_one(double x) {
  Scalar s{x};
  Scalar one{1.0};
  auto expr = s * s + one;
  return expr.eval();
}

static_assert(square_plus_one(3.0) == 10.0);
\end{code}

For this to compile, the leaf type must itself be a literal type: its
constructor and
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} must be
\texttt{constexpr}, and it must have no non-literal members. The
\texttt{Scalar} type shown earlier satisfies this with its defaulted
default constructor, its \texttt{constexpr} value constructor, and its
\texttt{constexpr}
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
\texttt{VecDSL} type using \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}array}
is also a literal type, since
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}array} of literal types is itself
literal.

Compile-time evaluation is not the primary motivation for expression
templates, but it is a consequence of the value-based,
\texttt{constexpr}-friendly design. Where a computation can be hoisted
to compile time, the machinery allows it without changes to the
expression syntax.

\section{Leaves With Mixed Operand
Types}\label{ch15-leaves-with-mixed-operand-types}

The operator templates accept any type \texttt{R} as the right operand.
This permits expressions where the two operands of a binary operation
have different types --- for example a \texttt{Scalar} plus a plain
\texttt{double}, or a \texttt{Scalar} plus a \texttt{BinExpr}.

\begin{code}
Scalar a{2}, b{3};
auto e1 = a + 5.0;                  // BinExpr<plus<>, Scalar, double>
auto e2 = a + b * 2.0;             // BinExpr<plus<>, Scalar, BinExpr<multiplies<>, Scalar, double>>
\end{code}

Evaluation resolves each operand through
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand}. A \texttt{Scalar} resolves via
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}; a
\texttt{double} resolves directly, since it has neither
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} nor
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
operation tag
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is then applied to the resulting pair. As long as the operation is
defined for the resolved value types, the expression is well-formed.

This flexibility is valuable when a DSL must interoperate with raw
numeric types. A user can write \texttt{a * 2.\allowbreak{}0 + b}
without wrapping the literal \texttt{2.\allowbreak{}0} in a
\texttt{Scalar}, and the resulting tree still fuses to a single
evaluation pass. The cost is that the type of \texttt{e1} mentions
\texttt{double} as an operand, which can complicate generic code that
assumes uniform operand types.

\section{The Friend Operator Pattern}\label{ch15-the-friend-operator-pattern}

The binary operators live inside the
\texttt{ExprTemplates:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin} as
\texttt{friend} function templates. This is a deliberate C++ idiom. A
\texttt{friend} function defined inside a class template is only
instantiated when found via argument-dependent lookup, and only for the
specific template arguments that match a call. The effect is that each
DSL type \texttt{Derived} gets its own set of operators, found only when
an operand of type \texttt{Derived} appears.

Without the \texttt{friend} trick, the operators would be ordinary free
function templates visible in the enclosing namespace. They would then
participate in overload resolution for every arithmetic expression in
the program, including those that have nothing to do with MuDSL,
producing ambiguity errors and surprising diagnostics. By embedding the
operators in the mixin as \texttt{friend}s, MuDSL ensures they are
considered only for expressions involving a \texttt{Derived} type.

The free-function overloads for \texttt{BinExpr} left operands are
ordinary templates in the \texttt{dsl} namespace. They are found via ADL
on the \texttt{BinExpr} argument, which lives in \texttt{dsl}. Because
\texttt{BinExpr} is exclusively a MuDSL type, these overloads do not
pollute the overload sets of unrelated arithmetic.

\section{Why Laziness Matters:
Fusion}\label{ch15-why-laziness-matters-fusion}

The central performance argument for expression templates is fusion. An
eager evaluation of \texttt{a + b * c} over vectors allocates one
temporary for the product, another for the sum, and discards both. A
fused evaluation walks the tree once, reading each leaf, performing the
operations in post-order, and writing the final result directly. No
intermediate is materialized.

For a single expression of two operators the saving is modest. For a
chain of ten operators over large vectors, the saving is substantial:
ten allocations become one, and the data passes through cache once
instead of ten times. The tree walk is recursive, but the recursion
depth equals the operator count, which is small and bounded by the
source expression.

Fusion is the topic of Chapter 16, which traces
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} in detail. The present chapter's
concern is the construction side: ensuring that the tree exists, that
its type encodes the operation, and that the operands are safely
captured. Construction is the prerequisite for fusion; without a tree
there is nothing to fuse.

\section{Composition With Other
Features}\label{ch15-composition-with-other-features}

\texttt{ExprTemplates} is one feature among many. A DSL type can mix in
several features simultaneously, and the expression-template operators
coexist with the pipeline, operator, rewrite, and other feature mixins
described in Chapters 4 through 14. The CRTP base
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived,\allowbreak{} Features.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
linearizes each feature's mixin, so the operators from
\texttt{ExprTemplates} appear alongside the predicate operators from
\texttt{Operators} and the \texttt{rewrite} method from
\texttt{Rewrite}.

Care is needed when feature sets overlap. The \texttt{Operators} feature
(Chapter 7) also provides \texttt{operator\&\&}, \texttt{operator||},
and \texttt{operator!} for predicate composition. These operate on a
different value domain --- boolean predicates --- and do not collide
with the arithmetic operators of \texttt{ExprTemplates}. A DSL that
mixes both features gains both operator sets, and the compiler selects
between them by argument type.

A DSL type that wishes to expose both symbolic arithmetic and predicate
composition would therefore list both features:

\begin{code}
struct MyDSL : dsl::DSL<MyDSL, dsl::ExprTemplates, dsl::Operators> {
  double v{};
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
  bool operator()(int x) const { return x > 0; }
};
\end{code}

The resulting type can participate in arithmetic expression trees
\emph{and} in predicate composition. The two domains are kept separate
by the types of the values involved:
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns
the arithmetic value, while
\texttt{operator(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns the predicate
value. This separation is a consequence of the value-agnostic design of
the node templates.

\section{A Larger Worked Example}\label{ch15-a-larger-worked-example}

To consolidate the material, consider a slightly larger expression that
exercises every operator and a unary negation.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

int main() {
  Scalar a{10}, b{3}, c{2}, d{5};
  auto expr = a + b * c - d / a + (-b);
  std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // 10 + 6 - 0.5 + (-3) = 12.5
}
\end{code}

The expression
\texttt{a + b * c - d /\allowbreak{} a + (\allowbreak{}-b)\allowbreak{}}
builds a tree with five binary nodes and one unary node. Reading the
type from the inside out: \texttt{b * c} is a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
\texttt{d /\allowbreak{} a} is a
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}divides\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
\texttt{-b} is a
\texttt{UnaryExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}negate\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The additions and subtraction then nest these as operands, producing a
left-leaning tree whose root is the final \texttt{+}.

Evaluation walks the tree once. Each leaf resolves to a \texttt{double}
via \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, each
inner node applies its operation tag, and the final result is
\texttt{12.\allowbreak{}5}. No intermediate \texttt{double} is exposed
to the caller; the entire computation is fused into the single
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} call.

\section{Pitfall: Temporaries and
Lifetime}\label{ch15-pitfall-temporaries-and-lifetime}

The most common error with expression templates is building an
expression that references a temporary. Because \texttt{BinExpr} stores
its operands by value, this is less hazardous than with reference-based
expression templates, but the related pitfall remains when the leaf type
itself holds references or pointers.

Consider a leaf that wraps a reference to an external container. If the
container is a temporary, the leaf dangles after the temporary is
destroyed, and the expression that contains the leaf dangles with it.
The expression-template machinery cannot prevent this; it can only
ensure that the nodes themselves own their immediate operands. The leaf
type is responsible for its own lifetime invariants.

A related concern arises when an expression is stored in a variable and
the leaves it was built from are later destroyed. Because the nodes copy
their operands, the expression survives the destruction of the originals
--- \emph{provided} the leaf type's copy is a deep copy. A leaf that
holds a \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector} by value copies
cleanly; a leaf that holds a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\&} does not. The rule of
thumb is the same as for any value type: leaves should be value types,
or should manage shared state explicitly.

\begin{code}
Scalar make_temp() { return Scalar{42}; }
auto bad = make_temp() + Scalar{1};   // OK: nodes hold copies, not references
\end{code}

In the snippet above the expression is safe, because \texttt{BinExpr}
stores its operands by value and \texttt{Scalar} is a value type. The
temporary \texttt{Scalar\{42\}} is moved into the node's left member,
and the temporary \texttt{Scalar\{1\}} is moved into the right member;
both temporaries are then destroyed, but their contents live on inside
the node. This is the principal advantage of value-based storage over
reference-based storage.

\section{Pitfall: The Type Is
Complex}\label{ch15-pitfall-the-type-is-complex}

The type of an expression is a nesting of \texttt{BinExpr} and
\texttt{UnaryExpr} instantiations that mirrors the source expression.
For anything beyond a few operators, the type is too long to write
conveniently and too specific to anticipate. The idiomatic remedy is
\texttt{auto}.

\begin{code}
auto e = a + b * c;        // good: type deduced
// BinExpr<plus<>, Scalar, BinExpr<multiplies<>, Scalar, Scalar>> e2 = a + b * c;
//                                       ^ impractical to write
\end{code}

Where the type must be named --- as a function return type, a class
member, or a template parameter --- use \texttt{decltype} or a trailing
return type. A function that builds and returns an expression can
declare its return type as
\texttt{decltype(\allowbreak{}a + b * c)\allowbreak{}}:

\begin{code}
auto make_expr(const Scalar& a, const Scalar& b, const Scalar& c) {
  return a + b * c;
}
\end{code}

Here the return type is deduced from the \texttt{return} statement, and
the caller uses \texttt{auto} to bind the result. This style keeps the
source readable while preserving the full type information for the
compiler.

Type erasure is sometimes appropriate when an expression must cross an
API boundary that cannot expose the full type. A
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textless{}\allowbreak{}double(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
wrapping
\texttt{[e]\{ return e.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}; \}}
erases the expression type at the cost of a heap allocation and an
indirect call. MuDSL does not provide such an erasure itself, but the
regular value semantics of the node types make it straightforward to
add.

\section{Pitfall: Assignment Does Not Rebuild the
Tree}\label{ch15-pitfall-assignment-does-not-rebuild-the-tree}

Because each expression has a distinct type, an expression variable
cannot be reassigned to a different shape. \texttt{auto e = a + b;}
gives \texttt{e} the type
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}plus\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
a later \texttt{e = a * b;} is ill-formed because \texttt{a * b} has
type
\texttt{BinExpr\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}multiplies\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} Scalar,\allowbreak{} Scalar\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The two types are unrelated despite their structural similarity.

When a program needs to vary the shape of an expression at runtime, the
usual approach is to select among pre-built expressions of the same
type, or to erase the type as described above. Alternatively, the leaf
type can be made to carry a runtime tag that selects the operation, and
a single fixed expression shape can evaluate to different results based
on that tag. These are workarounds for the fact that expression
templates are fundamentally a compile-time technique.

\section{Pitfall: Overload Ambiguity}\label{ch15-pitfall-overload-ambiguity}

The free-function overloads for \texttt{BinExpr} left operands accept
any \texttt{R} as the right operand. When \texttt{R} is itself a
\texttt{BinExpr}, the result is a deeper tree, which is usually what is
wanted. When \texttt{R} is a type that also has its own arithmetic
operators --- for example a type from another library --- overload
resolution may become ambiguous.

The remedy is to ensure that the right operand is either a MuDSL leaf, a
MuDSL node, or a plain value type that does not have competing
\texttt{operator+} overloads found via ADL. Mixing expression-template
types from different libraries in a single expression is generally not
supported without explicit glue. Within a single MuDSL-based program,
the operators are designed to compose without ambiguity.

\section{Inspecting a Tree at Compile
Time}\label{ch15-inspecting-a-tree-at-compile-time}

Although MuDSL does not provide a dedicated metaprogramming interface
for expression trees, the node types are simple enough to introspect
directly. A generic trait can detect \texttt{BinExpr} specializations,
extract their operation tag and operand types, and recurse.

\begin{code}
template <typename T> struct ExprShape;
template <typename Op, typename L, typename R>
struct ExprShape<dsl::BinExpr<Op, L, R>> {
  using op = Op;
  using left = L;
  using right = R;
};
\end{code}

Such a trait can be used to write algorithms that walk the type at
compile time --- for example, to verify that an expression contains no
division, or to count the number of operation nodes. The header does not
impose any particular trait; users are free to define their own. The
\texttt{ExprTerminal} tag type is provided as a marker for terminal
nodes should a metaprogram wish to distinguish them, though the leaf
types themselves are not required to derive from it.

This kind of introspection is rarely needed in application code, but it
is the foundation of more advanced techniques such as automatic
differentiation or symbolic simplification built on top of MuDSL
expression trees.

\section{Relationship to Chapter 16}\label{ch15-relationship-to-chapter-16}

This chapter has concentrated on tree construction. The companion
Chapter 16 covers the other half of the design: the
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member functions, the
\texttt{eval\_\allowbreak{}operand} dispatch helper, and the fusion
behavior that makes a single walk replace a chain of eager computations.
Readers who have understood the construction side will find the
evaluation side straightforward; the tree is already in place, and
evaluation is a recursive walk that applies each operation tag in
post-order.

The two chapters are deliberately split so that construction --- the
part visible to the DSL author who writes \texttt{a + b * c} --- is
separate from evaluation --- the part visible to the runtime that calls
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. Most users interact only with
construction. The evaluation internals matter for understanding
performance characteristics and for extending the machinery to new
operation tags.

\section{Summary}\label{ch15-summary}

Expression templates capture arithmetic expressions as compile-time
trees of operation nodes. The \texttt{ExprTemplates} feature tag injects
arithmetic operators into a DSL type via its CRTP mixin; the operators
return \texttt{BinExpr} and \texttt{UnaryExpr} nodes rather than
computed values. The \texttt{ExprLike} concept documents the contract a
leaf type must fulfill: mix in \texttt{ExprTemplates} and expose
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The node
types store their operands by value, which makes expressions safe to
store, return, and copy without lifetime concerns, at the cost of
copying the leaves into the tree. The type of an expression encodes its
shape, making \texttt{auto} the idiomatic binding for expression
variables. Because no arithmetic is performed at construction time, a
later \texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} can fuse the entire
computation into a single pass --- the subject of Chapter 16. The same
machinery serves scalars, vectors, and any user-defined value type, and
the \texttt{constexpr} design permits compile-time construction and
evaluation when the leaf types are literal types. The principal pitfalls
are the complexity of the resulting types, the value-semantics
requirement on leaves, and the impossibility of reassigning an
expression to a different shape without type erasure.
